I have confirmed this has been fixed in v1.30.4, not v1.30.3.
@Danil-Grigorev

So this seems to be the containerd issue. I don't think there is anything kubelet can do differently.

This sounds critical enough to warrant the cherry-pick to 1.x branch.

@samuelkarp @mikebrow wdyt?

@xuzhenglun , what I can share as below:
When one go application with go version: "go1.20.14" invokes kubectl v1.30.0 which is with goVersion: go1.22.2, to execute "kubectl cp" command to copy some folder to temporary OCP pod, the task failed with ERROR.
But the same task works with kubectl v1.29.3 (goVersion: go1.21.8) or kubectl v1.28.0 (goVersion: go1.20.7)

@wedaly joined us on the call today, and @aojea confirmed that we should move forward with the patch he proposed:

/triage accepted
/assign @wedaly

What's the proposal then? Do we update --node-monitor-grace-period to 50s?

It seems the less risky change as the client-go defaults are more sensitive and impact the whole ecosystem, beyond kubernetes itself 

@dchen1107 are you open to increase this default? It seems worth it to improve the reliability of cluster

I have been following this discussion as we had encountered the bug as well.
@aojea @rtheis  I have created a PR - https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/126287
Please let me know if it works

@SergeyKanzhelev Thanks for referencing that other issue, I hadn't seen it. I'm not acquainted with the inner workings well enough to know if this problem here is caused by the same error handling code paths analyzed there, but the two issues look very similar. Yes, this issue here is also about command probes.

One major difference here is that it's a condition that we, as the Kubernetes users, cannot fix. We're not configuring an invalid probe, but the (Windows containers) system stops being able to run our correctly configured probes for some reason. And it's not even our Pod, but one of AKS's. So just warning wouldn't be enough, we really need the restart so the system can heal.

I'll add a corresponding comment to the other issue. Should we close this one, then, or keep it open as it has a slightly different scope?
